===Etymology 1===
{{root|en|ine-pro|*megʰ-}}
From {{inh|en|enm|mayn}}, {{m|enm|main}}, {{m|enm|maine}}, {{m|enm|mæin}}, {{m|enm|meyn}}, from {{m|enm|main|pos=noun}} (''see further'' at [[#Etymology 2|etymology 2]]); compare {{inh|en|ang|mæġen||strong, main, principal}} {{qualifier|used in combination}}<ref>{{R:MED Online|entry=main|pos=adj|id=MED26523|accessdate=3 June 2018}}</ref> and {{der|en|non|megn}}, {{m|non|megenn||strong, main}}.

The word is cognate with {{cog|goh|megīn||strong, mighty}} (modern {{cog|de|Möge}}, {{m|de|Vermögen||power, wealth}}), and also akin to {{cog|ang|magan||to be able to}}. ''See also'' {{m|en|may}}.

====Adjective====
{{en-adj|-}}

# Of [[chief#Adjective|chief]] or [[leading#Adjective|leading]] [[importance]]; [[prime#Adjective|prime]], [[principal#Adjective|principal]]. {{defdate|from 15th c.}}
#: {{ux|en|The '''main''' office is actually one of the smaller rooms.}}
#* {{RQ:Tillotson Works|sermon=I|passage=''Religion'' direct us rather to ſecure ''inward peace'' than ''outward eaſe'', to be more careful to avoid ''everlaſting'' and ''intolerable torment'' than  ''ſhort'' and ''light afflictions which are but for a moment''; {{...}} In a word, our '''''main''' intereſt'' is to be ''as happy as we can'', and ''as long as is poſſible''; and if we be caſt into ſuch circumſtances, that we muſt be either in part and for a time or elſe wholly and always miſerable, the ''beſt wiſdom'' is to chuſe the ''greateſt'' and ''moſt laſting happiness'', but the ''leaſt'' and ''ſhorteſt miſery''.}}
#* {{RQ:Maxwell Mirror and the Lamp|chapter=VII|page=77|passage=With some of it on the south and more of it on the north of the great '''main''' thoroughfare that connects Aldgate and the East India Docks, St. Bede's at this period of its history was perhaps the poorest and most miserable parish in the East End of London.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|author=[[w:George Goodchild|[George Goodchild]]]|chapter=5|title=Death on the Centre Court; a McLean Mystery|location=London|publisher=[[w:Hodder & Stoughton|Hodder and Stoughton]]|year=1935|oclc=80449799|passage=By one o'clock the place was choc-a-bloc. {{...}} The restaurant was packed, and the promenade between the two '''main''' courts and the subsidiary courts was thronged with healthy-looking youngish people, drawn to the Mecca of tennis from all parts of the country.}}
# Chief, most [[important]], or principal in [[extent]], [[size#Noun|size]], or [[strength]]; consisting of the [[largest]] part.
#: {{synonyms|en|largest}}
#: {{ux|en|'''main''' timbers}}
#: {{ux|en|'''main''' branch of a river}}
#: {{ux|en|'''main''' body of an army}}
#* {{RQ:Milton Paradise Lost|book=VI|page=170|lines=470–471|passage=Not uninvented that, which thou aright / Beleivſt ſo '''main''' to our ſucceſs, I bring; {{...}}|year=1873}}
#* {{RQ:Economist|title=The Future of Oil: Yesterday’s fuel|url=https://www.economist.com/leaders/2013/08/03/yesterdays-fuel|archiveurl=https://web.archive.org/web/20130801154742/http://www.economist.com/news/leaders/21582516-worlds-thirst-oil-could-be-nearing-peak-bad-news-producers-excellent|archivedate=1 August 2013|date=3 August 2013|volume=408|issue=8847|passage=The dawn of the oil age was fairly recent. Although the stuff was used to waterproof boats in the Middle East 6,000 years ago, extracting it in earnest began only in 1859 after an oil strike in Pennsylvania. {{...}} It was used to make kerosene, the '''main''' fuel for artificial lighting after overfishing led to a shortage of whale blubber. Other liquids produced in the refining process, too unstable or smoky for lamplight, were burned or dumped.}}
# {{lb|en|nautical}} Belonging to or connected with the principal [[mast]] in a [[vessel]].
# {{lb|en|archaic|of force, strength, etc.}} [[full|Full]], [[sheer]], [[undivided]]. {{defdate|from 16th c.}}
#* {{RQ:Scott Rob Roy|volume=I|page=281|passage={{...}} I shall never forget the diabolical sneer which writhed Rashleigh's wayward features, as I was forced from the apartment by the '''main''' strength of two of these youthful Titans.}}
#* {{RQ:Scott Tales of the Crusaders|volume=I|chapter=IV|page=71|passage=Wounded and overthrown, the Britons continued their resistance, clung round the legs of the Norman steeds, and cumbered their advance; while their brethren, thrusting with pikes, proved every joint and crevice of the plate and mail, or grappling with the men-at-arms, strove to pull them from their horses by '''main''' force, or beat them down with their bills and Welch hooks.}}
# {{lb|en|Appalachia}} {{1|extraordinary}}, [[exceptional]].
# {{lb|en|dialectal}} [[big|Big]]; [[angry]]. {{attn|en|which dialects?}} {{rfex|en}}
# {{lb|en|obsolete}} [[great#Adjective|Great]] in size or [[degree]]; [[important]], [[powerful]], [[strong]], [[vast]].
#* {{quote-book|en|author=w:Samuel Daniel|chapter=The History of the Civil War. Book V.|title=The Poetical Works of Mr. Samuel Daniel, Author of the English History.{{nb...|To which is Prefix’d, Memoirs of His Life and Writings.}}|location=London|publisher=Printed for R. Gosling,{{nb...|against St. Dunstan's Church in Fleetstreet;}} W. Mears,{{nb...|at the Lamb,}} and J. Browne{{nb...|at the Black Swan, without Temple-Bar}}|year=1718|volume=II|section=stanza LXXXIX|page=167|pageurl=https://books.google.com/books?id=qx4JAAAAQAAJ&pg=PA167|oclc=1904801|passage=And now that Current with '''main''' Fury ran / (The Stop remov'd that did the Courſe defend) / Unto the full of Miſchief, that began / T' an univerſal Ruin to extend; {{...}}}}

