===Etymology 2===
From {{inh|en|enm|mayn}}, {{m|enm|main}}, {{m|enm|maine}}, {{m|enm|mæine}}, {{m|enm|mæȝen}}, from {{inh|en|ang|mæġen||strength}},<ref>{{R:MED Online|entry=main|pos=n|id=MED26522|accessdate=3 June 2018}}</ref> from {{inh|en|gem-pro|*maginą||strength, power, might}}, {{m|gem-pro|*maginaz||strong}}, from {{der|en|ine-pro|*megʰ-||be able}}.

The word is cognate with {{cog|goh|magen}}, {{m|goh|megin}}, {{cog|non|magn}}, {{m|non|megn}}, {{m|non|megin}}, {{cog|osx|megin}}.<ref>{{R:OED2|entry=main, ''sb.''{{sup|1}}|page=216|columns=1–2}}</ref> More recent senses are derived from the adjective.

====Noun====
{{en-noun}}

# {{lb|en|not in generic modern use}} That which is [[chief#Adjective|chief]] or [[principal#Adjective|principal]]; the chief or main [[portion]]; the [[bulk#Noun|bulk]], the [[greater]] [[part#Noun|part]], [[gross#Noun|gross]].
#* {{quote-book|en|author=w:Humphrey Prideaux|title=The Old and New Testaments Connected in the History of the Jews and Neighbouring Nations, from the Declension of the Kingdoms of Israel and Judah to the Time of Christ|edition=3rd|location=London|publisher=Printed for R. Knaplock{{nb...|at the Bishop's Head in St. Paul's Church-Yard,}} and [[w:Jacob Tonson|J[acob] Tonson]]{{nb...|at Shakespear's Head over-against Katharine-Street in the Strand}}|year=1718|volume=II, part I|section=part II, book II|page=96|pageurl=https://books.google.com/books?id=rU6mWbx_FogC&pg=PA96|oclc=695990865|passage=''[[w:Antiochus III the Great|Antiochus]]'' {{...}} thought it a proper time for him to attempt the recovery of ''Syria''; and ''[[w:Hermeias|Hermias]]'' his prime Miniſter preſſed hard for his going in perſon to this war, contrary to the Opinion of ''Epigenes'' his General; who thought it chiefly concerned him to ſuppreſs the Rebellion of ''[[w:Alexander (satrap)|Alexander]]'' and ''{{w|Molon}}'' in the East; and therefore adviſed him to march immediately in perſon with the '''main''' of his Army for the ſubduing of thoſe Rebels, before they ſhould gather greater ſtrength in the revolted Provinces againſt him.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|author=w:Francis Bacon|chapter=The History of the Reign of King Henry the Seventh|title=The Works of Francis Bacon, Baron of Verulam, Viscount St. Alban, and Lord High Chancellor of England. In Ten Volumes|volume=V|location=London|publisher=Printed for J. Johnson [''et al.''];{{nb...|by C. and R. Baldwin, New Bridge-street}}|year=1803|page=8|pageurl=https://archive.org/stream/b29321712_0005#page/8/mode/1up|oclc=5861323|passage=But the King {{q-q|{{w|Henry VII of England}}}}, {{...}} preferring his affection to his own line and blood, {{...}} resolved to rest upon the title of Lancaster as the '''main''', and to use the other two, that of marriage, and that of battle, but as supporters, the one to appease secret discontents, and the other to beat down open murmur and dispute; {{...}}}}
