#: {{ux|en|I had scampi and chips for my '''main''' and a slice of cheesecake for dessert.}}
# {{lb|en|now|poetic}} The [[high seas]]. {{defdate|from 16th c.}}
#* {{RQ:Spenser Faerie Queene|book=II|canto=VI|stanza=17|page=261|passage=Who ſhall him [[rue|rew]], that ſwimming in the '''maine''', / Will die for [[thrist|thriſt]], and water doth refuſe? / Refuſe ſuch fruitleſſe toile, and preſent pleaſures chuſe.}}
#* {{RQ:Dryden Virgil|chapter=The Fifth Book of the [[w:Aeneid|Æneis]]|page=360|lines=1115–1119|passage=The God, inſulting with ſuperiour Strength, / Fell heavy on him, plung'd him in the Sea, / And, with the Stern, the Rudder tore away, / Headlong he fell, and, ſtrugling in the '''Main''', / Cry'd out for helping hands, but cry'd in vain: {{...}}}}
#* {{quote-book|en|author=[[w:Thomas Broughton (writer)|Thomas Broughton]] (libretto); {{w|George Frideric Handel}} (music)|chapter=[[w:Hercules (Handel)|Hercules: An Oratorio]]|title=The Miscellaneous Pieces, as Set to Music, of Geo. Fred. Handel.{{nb...|[...] With the Life of Handel, and a General Index}}|location=London|publisher=Printed for T. Heptinstall,{{nb...|No. 304, Holborn; and sold by H. D. Symonds [et al.]}}|year=c. 1744|year_published=1799|volume_plain=part II|section=part the second [Act II, scene iv]|page=53|pageurl=https://books.google.com/books?id=xvZdAAAAcAAJ&pg=PA53|oclc=642364001|passage=Wanton god of am'rous fires, / Wishes, sighs and soft desires, / All nature's sons thy laws maintain; / O'er liquid air, firm land, and swelling '''main''', / Extend thy uncontroul'd and boundless reign.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|author=w:Rudyard Kipling|chapter=w:The Sons of Martha|title=Rudyard Kipling’s Verse: Inclusive Edition 1885–1918|location=London|publisher=[[w:Hodder & Stoughton|Hodder and Stoughton Ltd.]]|year=1907|year_published=1927|pages=436–437|pageurl=https://archive.org/stream/in.ernet.dli.2015.34375/2015.34375.Rudyard-Kiplings-Verse1885-1918#page/n455/mode/1up|oclc=5198131|passage=The Sons of Mary seldom bother, for they have inherited that good part; / But the Sons of Martha favour their Mother of the careful soul and the troubled heart, / {{...}} / It is their care that the wheels run truly; it is their care to embark and entrain, / Tally, transport, and deliver duly the Sons of Mary by land and '''main'''.}}
# {{lb|en|now|archaic|US|_|dialectal}} The [[mainland]]. {{defdate|from 16th c.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|author=w:Francis Bacon|chapter=Considerations Touching a War with Spain. Inscribed to Prince ''Charles'', An. 1624.|title=The Works of Francis Bacon, Baron of Verulam, Viscount St. Alban, and Lord High Chancellor of England|location=London|publisher=Printed for J[ohn] Walthoe,{{nb...|D. Midwinter, W[illiam] Innys, A[aron] Ward, D[aniel] Browne, C[harles] Davis, J[acob] and R[ichard] Tonson, and A[ndrew] Millar}}|year=1624|year_published=1740|volume=III|page=526|pageurl=https://archive.org/stream/worksoffrancisba03baco#page/526/mode/1up|oclc=960099509|passage=In the year that followed of 1589, we gave the ''Spaniards'' no breath, but turned challengers, invaded the '''main''' of ''Spain''. In which enterprize, although we failed of our end, which was to ſettle ''[[w:António, Prior of Crato|Don Antonio]]'' in the kingdom of ''Portugal'', yet a man ſhall hardly meet with an action that doth better reveal the great ſecret of the power of ''Spain'': {{...}}}}
#* {{RQ:Donne Devotions|chapter=17. Meditation|pages=415–416|pageref=416|passage=No man is an ''Iland'', intire of it ſelfe; euery man is a peece of the ''Continent'', a part of the '''''maine'''''; {{...}}}}
#* {{RQ:Smith Generall Historie|authors=Anthony Bagnall; Nathanaell Powell; Anas Todkill|chapter=Chapter V. The Accidents that Hapned in the Discovery of the Bay of Chisapeack|page=56|passage=The higheſt land on the '''mayne''', yet it was but low, we called ''Keales'' hill, and theſe vninhabited Iſles, ''Ruſſels Iſles''.}}
